A||N||7||Monkeyman||Lots of travel in the gas pedal!!!!||||1||
Z||000000||Monkeyman||02-24-2000||07:34 PM||||I've noticed lately that there seems to be lots of travel in the gas pedal.  I haven't measured it, but I would guess it was a 1/4" when I first noticed it.  Now it's more like a full inch or more.  I need to be able to drive my car to work every day so I don't want it to die on me.  What should I look for, what could cause this, and what do I need to do to fix it?????????!!!!!!!!  BTW--It's an '86 SC with the 4/auto.||||reg||1
Z||000001||mshill||02-24-2000||07:39 PM||||More travel... doesn't that mean that you can go faster now.||||reg||7
Z||000002||theogre||02-24-2000||07:40 PM||||sounds like the cable is comming apart. I think the only return for the peddal is the spring in back on the motor. I can't remeber right now. I don't think there's one on the peddal shaft. just one on the plastic pedal to keep it from flopping around.<P>Check the TBI. if the screws are loose on the throttle shaft it could slop. worse you don't want a loose screw in the butterfly.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Monkeyman||02-24-2000||08:04 PM||||What would it take to fix that, ogre (or anybody else).  How much and what would I need to do?||||reg||1
Z||000004||Richard Parnell||02-24-2000||08:20 PM||||Along the same line, I noticed something odd the other day.  I had someone push the gas pedal all the way down (engine off) while I watched the linkage move.  With the gas pedal fully depressed I could reach in and still move the throttle another inch or so.  Does this mean that I have not got full throttle when driving pedal to the metal?  If true, all this time I coulda been going faster.||||reg||5
Z||000005||Monkeyman||02-24-2000||08:28 PM||||Just looked at the car.  It's quite possible that I'm dumber than a box of rocks and twice as stupid.  I just happened to go out in my slippers.  I looked at the TBI, etc and could find nothing wrong with the linkage.  Then I sat in the car and pushed on the gas (didn't know what else to do).  I could feel the pedal move when I had my foot fully on the pedal.  Then I realized that what I felt move was actually the pedal rocking on the rod that holds it in.  How stupid can I be?!  (I don't need an answer to that!)  I always wear boots of one kind or another so I could never feel the pedal rock until tonight.<BR>This brings me to another problem/question.  I noticed that there is a vacuum line (I think) that has broken and was taped up.  It runs out of the sensor on the right side of the air filter housing (can you tell I'm not a mechanic? <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">).  What is this sensor, and what does the vacuum line (if that's what it is) do?  Would it make my car idle rough?  (Low idle when in gear and warmed up-tends to stall if I don't give it some gas.)  Maybe I've found my idle problem.  Someone let me know.  TIA||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||02-24-2000||11:11 PM||||Normally. There should be a vac line from a manifold or a TBI port, I forget which 87 doesn't use this, to a temperature activated valve on the air cleaner. from that valve the line runs to the Thermac actuator on the air cleaner neck.<P>There is also a sensor hanging off the outside of the air cleaner, usually on the right, that has 1 vac line and a 3 wire weather pack connector. That is the MAP sensor. This vac line must go to a specific port on the TBI. If you cannot figure out what port on the TBI then it must go directly to manifold vacuum. If MAP doesn't work generally the engine runs like hell at all times. It won't just idle bad.<P>The vac ports on the TBI go to diferant vacuum sources, and are calibrated sizes. It does matter what line connects to what port. Incorrect conection will cause problems. The VECI lable on the deck lid will show you what goes where. <P>for example at least one port is routed to Ventury vacuum above the butterfly. others are metered size manifold vac. The location and size of port controls things like when and how fast the EGR opens and when the vapor canister will purge. One manifold port connects to the actual canister purge line another to the MAP.<P>If a hard vac line is broken it can be replaced as long as the rubber parts are good. otherwise replace it with soft line.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Standard||02-25-2000||12:34 AM||||my 84 has that pedal "play" too.  disconcerting at times, cause the throttle cable is pretty stiff in that car, probably corroded somewhere... <P>If the sensor is hanging on the outside of the air cleaner, with an electrical connector, it's the map sensor.||||reg||1
